Date: Mon, 08 Oct 2012 15:07:33 +0000
Subject: Sorting a data frame with ddply instead of arrange
Message-ID: <V-10gBLE_kY.rzmNbNc4NngJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/manipulatr/c/V-10gBLE_kY/m/rzmNbNc4NngJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi All,

I just stumbled across the fact that you can sort a data frame using ddply instead of arrange. If you use ddply and don't give it a function to apply, it simply splits the data frame by your variables and combines the pieces having done nothing to them. But since it does this in order, you've ended up sorting. In hindsight this is a bit obvious but I got a chuckle out of it so I thought I'd share. Example below.

Cheers,
Bob

> group <- sample(c("a","b"), 10, replace=TRUE)
> x <- rnorm(10)
> df <- data.frame(group,x)
> df
   group        x
1      a  1.79599
2      b -0.14837
3      a  0.16792
4      a -0.70860
5      b -0.52039
6      b -0.54596
7      b  1.71299
8      b  1.90250
9      b -0.37526
10     b -0.83634
> arrange(df, group, x)
   group        x
1      a -0.70860
2      a  0.16792
3      a  1.79599
4      b -0.83634
5      b -0.54596
6      b -0.52039
7      b -0.37526
8      b -0.14837
9      b  1.71299
10     b  1.90250
> ddply(df, .(group, x))
   group        x
1      a -0.70860
2      a  0.16792
3      a  1.79599
4      b -0.83634
5      b -0.54596
6      b -0.52039
7      b -0.37526
8      b -0.14837
9      b  1.71299
10     b  1.90250

