Hi Hadley,

Since the default setting in failwith is "default = NULL", I tried skipping that setting by naming the f argument:

results <- dlply(mydata, .(group), failwith(f=myT))

That didn't work, but shouldn't it have?

While the failwith solution to this particular problem is very useful, having a ".na.rm" argument in the d*ply functions would be helpful for cases that don't result in errors but for which you just don't want the results for missing ".variable" levels. I suppose the dot argument ".na.rm" for d*ply could get confused with the non-dot version of "na.rm" for the function that's being applied.

Cheers,

Bob

